{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey leaves rent regulation to municipalities","requirement":"New Jersey has no state law that establishes, governs or controls rents; municipalities may adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances, which locally created boards enforce.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Statewide framework: local rent control ordinances cover residential rentals in municipalities that adopt them; subsidized rents and certain publicly financed developments may be exempt.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental units in municipalities with rent control or rent leveling ordinances"},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may be exempt; HUD-owned or HUD-subsidized developments, unsubsidized HUD-insured developments with economic problems, and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings, for 30 years following completion of construction.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"Under the Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5), newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"Exempts covered buildings from local rent control ordinances.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase notice and unconscionability standard","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease; to raise rent the landlord must terminate the old lease, give written notice and offer a new lease at the increased rent, and the increase must not be unconscionable.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants statewide; increases must comply with any municipal rent control ordinance and give notice to quit and notice of rent increase.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential dwelling units"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Conversion cost rent increase prohibition","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion; increases for new services or amenities are allowed.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a converted building, regardless of whether they are eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may evict or fail to renew a residential tenancy only for good cause proved in court; each cause except nonpayment of rent must be described in detail in a written Notice to Quit, with a Notice to Cease where required.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential dwelling units used as a residence; possession only by tenant consent or through the legal eviction process.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential dwelling units used as a residence"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer and return duty on sale of rental property","requirement":"When rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest from the former owner and is responsible for investing the deposit, giving notices, paying interest and returning the deposit with accrued earnings.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applies when a rental property is sold or transferred; special penalty protection for tenants receiving state or federal financial assistance, including rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Willful withholding of a deposit of a tenant receiving state or federal financial assistance: civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 per offense, enforced by summary proceeding, with amounts going to the State entity; unlawful use of deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense with a fine of at least $200 or up to 30 days' imprisonment.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"If the property is sold or transferred it is the duty of the new owner to obtain the security deposit, plus accrued interest on the tenant’s deposit, that was collected by the former owner.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination housing protections including source of lawful income","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in renting housing, and must treat applicants equally, regardless of protected characteristics including source of lawful income.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and residences planned for and occupied by one sex as to familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"All housing, with exceptions for small owner-occupied dwellings and single-sex residences"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, as it pertains to familial status.","penalty":"Under the LAD, landlords who violate the law are subject to fines and penalties up to $10,000 for a first offense (N.J.S.A. 10:5-14.1a(a)).","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination (LAD), N.J.S.A. 10:5-12(g) to -(h), prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
